; Project coverage across several test stanzas (see README.md): one
; shared instrumented library, two test executables each exercising
; their half, and the one rule that merges their .coverage dumps.
;
;   dune build @examples/09-coverage-aggregation/example-cover \
;     --instrument-with ppx_windtrap.coverage
;
; In your own project you would call this alias `cover`, which is the
; name windtrap's inline coverage line tells readers to type and the name
; windtrap's own root dune uses. It is `example-cover` HERE for one
; reason: this example lives inside windtrap's tree, `@cover` is
; recursive, and an alias called `cover` in a subdirectory would fire the
; project aggregate a second time with a different --min every time
; someone ran `dune build @cover` at the root.
;
; runs every stanza that is out of date, then aggregates. Plain
; `dune runtest` stays uninstrumented and coverage-free. (universe) is
; load-bearing: the .coverage files are not declarable dependencies, so
; it makes the milliseconds-cheap aggregate re-run on every build;
; without it the action caches against nothing and goes stale.

(library
 (name windtrap_example_aggregation)
 (modules half_a half_b)
 (instrumentation
  (backend ppx_windtrap.coverage)))

(test
 (name test_a)
 (modules test_a)
 (libraries windtrap windtrap_example_aggregation))

(test
 (name test_b)
 (modules test_b)
 (libraries windtrap windtrap_example_aggregation))

(rule
 (alias example-cover)
 (deps
  (alias_rec runtest)
  (universe))
 (action
  (setenv
   WINDTRAP_COVERAGE_FILE
   windtrap-reporter-self.coverage
   (run %{bin:windtrap} coverage --min 80))))
